---
import DocsCodeBlock from "../../components/DocsCodeBlock.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocsRelated from "../../components/DocsRelated.astro";

/* The user-facing half of capabilities. The manifest page already covers CONTRIBUTING a card; nothing covered
 * adding one, which is the step between "I have a sandbox" and "it can do my job". */

const env = `# Two Postgres connections, added as "analytics" and "billing".
POSTGRES_URL_ANALYTICS=postgres://…
POSTGRES_URL_BILLING=postgres://…

# The agent never guesses these: each connection's own skill file names its exact variables.`;

const skills = `.claude/skills/
├── analytics/SKILL.md     # how to query THIS database, with its variable names
├── github/SKILL.md        # the gh CLI, scoped to the token you gave it
└── ssh/SKILL.md           # every connected machine, in one cheatsheet`;

const categories = [
    { name: `Code & issues`, hint: `Repos, issues and pipelines as agent tools: GitHub, GitLab, Redmine.` },
    { name: `Observability`, hint: `Query errors, traces, logs and metrics: Sentry, SigNoz.` },
    { name: `Data`, hint: `Let the agent query your SQL databases: Postgres, MySQL.` },
    { name: `Communication`, hint: `Let the agent read and send messages: Discord, Slack, Telegram, WhatsApp, email.` },
    { name: `Business & docs`, hint: `Payments and knowledge bases: Stripe, Outline, npm.` },
    { name: `Your computers`, hint: `Let the agent work on a machine of your own: run commands, handle files, see the screen.` },
    { name: `Servers`, hint: `Remote machines over SSH, and private networks over VPN.` },
    { name: `Deploy & infra`, hint: `Drive container deployments: Docker, Komodo.` },
    { name: `Platform`, hint: `Scaffold managed repos that appear as their own operator panels.` },
    { name: `Extend`, hint: `Any MCP server, any Claude Code plugin, any model endpoint, any extension.` },
];

const shapes = [
    {
        name: `A command-line tool`,
        detail: `The agent gets the CLI on its PATH and your credential in its environment. This is most connectors: GitHub, a database, Sentry.`,
    },
    {
        name: `An MCP server`,
        detail: `Its tools appear to the agent directly, named after the connection. Point it at any MCP server, hosted or your own.`,
    },
    {
        name: `A logged-in browser`,
        detail: `For a platform whose API can't cover "all the actions". You sign in once, out of band; the profile persists and the agent drives that browser already signed in.`,
    },
    {
        name: `A machine`,
        detail: `A server over SSH, a private network over VPN, or one of your own computers dialling in. One capability is one machine, and its id is the name the agent addresses it by.`,
    },
];
---

<DocsLayout
    pageId="capabilities"
    heading="Capabilities"
    lead="Give an agent tools it can run, systems it can query and machines it can operate. Each connection takes one click, and its credential stays in your sandbox instead of your repo."
>
    <h2>What a fresh sandbox can't do yet</h2>
    <p>
        A fresh sandbox can read and write your code. It cannot yet look at your error tracker, query your database, open a pull request, or
        deploy anything. Capabilities are how you close that gap, one connection at a time, on the{" "}
        <strong>Capabilities</strong> page.
    </p>

    <h2>What you're actually adding</h2>
    <p>Four shapes, and which one a card uses is a detail of that integration rather than a choice you make:</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Shape</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                shapes.map((shape) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{shape.name}</strong></td>
                        <td>{shape.detail}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The catalog</h2>
    <p>The "+" grid is grouped by what a connection is <em>for</em>, not by how it works:</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Section</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                categories.map((category) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{category.name}</strong></td>
                        <td>{category.hint}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        Most connector cards come from installed extensions rather than being baked into the app, which is why the grid grows without an
        update, and why you can add your own. See <a href="/developers/manifest/">contributes.capabilities</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding one</h2>
    <p>
        Pick a card and you get two things side by side: a <strong>guide</strong> and a <strong>form</strong>. The guide is the part that usually costs the time: where to create the credential and which scopes it needs, with a deep link to the right settings page, at the provider or your own instance. The form is the fields that connection needs, secrets masked.
    </p>
    <p>
        Saving applies it, and you watch it happen: provisioning runs as a real terminal job with its output on screen, rather than a spinner
        that either turns green or doesn't. The card then reports one of four states: <strong>active</strong>, <strong>pending</strong>,{" "}
        <strong>error</strong> or <strong>inactive</strong>. The daemon probes it live, so "connected" means it just checked, not that it
        worked once.
    </p>
    <p>
        Some capabilities need something installed in the sandbox image: the browser one needs Chromium. Those bake a fragment into your
        environment overlay and sit <strong>pending</strong> until you approve the rebuild, because changing what the container contains is
        your call, not the extension's.
    </p>

    <h2>What the agent receives</h2>
    <p>Two things, both of them boring on purpose: an environment, and a cheatsheet.</p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={env} lang="bash" title="The agent's shell" />
    <p>
        Every variable is suffixed with the connection's own id, so a second connection of the same kind never collides with the first. That
        is what makes <em>two</em> databases, or a work GitHub and a personal one, a normal setup rather than a conflict.
    </p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={skills} lang="bash" title="In the workspace" />
    <p>
        Each connection also writes a skill file the agent loads automatically: what the tool is, how to drive it, and the exact variable
        names <em>this</em> connection uses. So the agent doesn't guess at credentials or invent flags: the thing that otherwise turns a
        connected tool into three wasted turns.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the credentials live</h2>
    <p>
        In your sandbox, in a file the agent is not allowed to read. The manifest of active capabilities sits in the workspace's control-plane directory and is on the daemon's secret denylist, so the file routes answer "not found". Neither an agent nor an extension can read its own credential store out through the file API.
    </p>
    <p>
        Nothing goes to the intentic platform. The platform holds your identity and your sandbox's address; every credential here is one your
        sandbox stores and your sandbox uses.
    </p>
    <p>
        Plain environment secrets, such as an API key your own code needs, go on <strong>Sandbox → Secrets</strong>{" "}
        instead. That page also has an inventory view that aggregates every store, showing keys and where they came from, never values.
    </p>

    <h2>Living with them</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Re-adding edits.</strong> Adding a connection under an id that already exists updates it in place rather than making a second one.</li>
        <li><strong>Removing tears down.</strong> The credential, the environment variables and the skill file all go.</li>
        <li><strong>One bad entry costs only itself.</strong> An unreadable stored connection is skipped and reported by name, whether it came from a hand edit or an old format. It is never rewritten, and every other capability keeps working.</li>
    </ul>

    <DocsRelated
        links={[
            { href: "/docs/automations/", title: "Automations & workflows", why: "have the agent act on what a connected system tells it." },
            { href: "/docs/autonomous-employees/", title: "Autonomous employees", why: "the narrative version: specialise one sandbox until it does a job on its own." },
        ]}
    />
</DocsLayout>
